mplate  TEST=unit tests BUG=none  Change-Id: I5e2fccbbd48bb01e6de0c6146a3727d4495fc3db Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/330921 Commit-Ready: Simon Que &lt;sque@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Simon Que &lt;sque@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: David Sharp &lt;dhsharp@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="330921" owner_email="sque@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/platform2" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform2" ref="refs/changes/21/330921/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="If13f0a2f0f22a29c831cdf7e49c4a3efee424d63" commit="2bff6de47f76e0f599f67852380ac57e05b089b5" commit_message="servod: Change EC-3PO PTYs to be owned by user.  Since the PTY permissions of the PTYs served by EC-3PO are 660, users often have to add themselves to the 'tty' group, or use sudo to open their serial consoles.  This isn't always desirable due to the fact that some users might have customizations or other settings that they want to use.  In order to make opening the PTYs be &quot;seamless&quot; as in the past, this commit changes the owner and group of the PTYs to be that of the user's which started servod by means of the sudo environment variables.  Additionally, there was a change to inherit from the Uart class instead of the FTDI Fuart class.  BUG=chromium:583528 BRANCH=None TEST=cros_workon start hdctools; sudo emerge hdctools; Start servod and check that the owner of the 'ec_uart_pty' is set to the user which started servod.  Change-Id: If13f0a2f0f22a29c831cdf7e49c4a3efee424d63 Signed-off-by: Aseda Aboagye &lt;aaboagye@google.com&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/330416 Commit-Ready: Aseda Aboagye &lt;aaboagye@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Aseda Aboagye &lt;aaboagye@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Mike Frysinger &lt;vapier@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="330416" owner_email="aaboagye@chromium.org" pass_count="